::ingredients

(egg choice) = (egg, whole, preferably room temperature) 1 -OR- (egg: yolk, preferably room temperature) 2
(oil, neutral) 2.5dl / 1 cup
(acid) = (vinegar) 1-2tbsp -OR- (lemon juice) 1-2tbsp
(pickle juice, optional) 1tbsp
(garlic, for aioli only) 2-4 cloves
(mustard, dijon, optional) 1tsp
# Method-only seasonings
(salt) 1 pinch
(pepper) 1 pinch
(spices, extra, optional) as desired
(vinegar, extra, optional) as desired

::equipment

(beaker) -OR- (container, narrow and tall)
(immersion blender)

::instructions

{base} = (egg choice) + (acid) + (salt) + (pepper)
Optional [
    {base} + (mustard)
]
{base} <place, beaker or narrow tall container>
Optional [
    (garlic) <dice>
    {base} + (garlic) <add, for aioli; omit for mayonnaise>
]
(oil) <add, very last, above other ingredients to avoid separation>
(immersion blender) <place, very bottom, not running yet>
{mayonnaise or aioli} <blend, full power, moving upwards slowly until all oil incorporated>
Optional [
    {mayonnaise or aioli} <taste>
        + (spices, extra) + (vinegar, extra) <mix>
]

::story

Aioli is pretty much just mayonnaise with garlic, although traditional aioli only includes olive oil and garlic, and no eggs or acid. This recipe uses raw eggs, so if that is sketchy in your region use pasteurized eggs 🥚.

